Denmark has no law against hitchhiking, and on ordinary roads it is legal. [https://www.retsinformation.dk/eli/lta/2024/1312 § 44 of the ''færdselslov''] restricts traffic on a ''motorvej'' — and expressly on its entry and exit roads — to motor vehicles that can and may travel at at least 50 km/h, which leaves a pedestrian nowhere lawful on the motorway, its hard shoulder or its slip roads. § 46(2) forbids stopping or parking anywhere on a motorway other than the signposted ''parkerings-'' and ''rastepladser'', excepting only a stop at an emergency telephone, and also bans turning and reversing there. So the rest areas and their petrol stations are where you ask drivers, and they are the only places one may legally stop for you. Thumb from the on-ramp before the motorway sign, and on ''hovedveje'' and smaller roads from the shoulder, freely.
